/**

\page page_tarch_mpi MPI programming

\tableofcontents

The tarch::mpi wrappers add return-value checks, timeout diagnostics, and
watched waits to MPI operations.

# Point-to-point communication

Reserve point-to-point tags through tarch::mpi::Rank::reserveFreeTag(). Two
variable-length payloads between the same ranks use distinct tags so a receive
cannot match the wrong message. Start a pairwise exchange only when both ranks
derive the same participants and payload size.

Non-blocking operations use tarch::mpi::Request, tarch::mpi::RequestGroup, or
the stack communication interface. The communication manager can poll
registered transfers on the calling thread or on the optional communication
thread. One MPI request has one poller.

# Collectives

The collective wrappers create a non-blocking MPI collective and complete its
watched wait inside the same call. Every rank in the communicator enters
collectives in the same order, and a collective is skipped only when all ranks
derive the same condition.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
double localValue = 1.0;
double globalValue = 0.0;

tarch::mpi::allreduce(
  &localValue,
  &globalValue,
  1,
  MPI_DOUBLE,
  MPI_MIN,
  tarch::mpi::Rank::getInstance().getCommunicator()
);
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

# Synchronisation

Use rank-local C++ synchronisation for threads and MPI collectives or
point-to-point messages for ranks. tarch::mpi::barrier() is available when the
algorithm requires an explicit communicator barrier.

*/
